EXT. ABANDONED MISSION CHURCH - COURTYARD - SUNSET

A crumbling adobe courtyard where a dry fountain stands at the center, cracked and filled with tumbleweed. A bell tower leans, its bell long gone. Long violet shadows crawl across the flagstones. Beyond the broken wall, the desert runs to a copper horizon.

CALEB MONTOYA-HART, sixties, a widowed rancher with a weathered hat and a silver mustache, sits on the rim of the fountain with a canteen across his knees. Beside him, his granddaughter ROSALIE, ten, in a faded blue dress and a straw hat too big for her, draws in the dust with a stick. A little way off, an old gray mule named SAINT CHRISTOPHER stands with his head low, and a stranger, a wandering priest called FATHER EMILIO DUARTE, fifties, in a patched cassock and sandals, kneels by a small fire, boiling coffee in a tin cup.

The desert is quiet. The coffee smells of smoke.

CALEB
Father, I didn't think there was anyone left out here.

FATHER EMILIO
There never is. And then there is.

ROSALIE
Grandpa, why is the fountain empty?

CALEB
Because there's no water.

ROSALIE
But why did they build it?

FATHER EMILIO
Because once there was.

ROSALIE
Where did it go?

FATHER EMILIO
(smiling)
That is a very good question, little one. The old ones said it went underground to rest.

ROSALIE
Water doesn't rest.

FATHER EMILIO
No? Everything rests, eventually.

Rosalie considers this. She draws a long wavy line in the dust, then a second beneath it.

CALEB
You've been here long?

FATHER EMILIO
Some years.

CALEB
Alone?

FATHER EMILIO
Never alone.

He lifts the cup from the fire, blows on it, and offers it to Caleb. Caleb studies it, takes it, and drinks. His shoulders ease. He passes it to Rosalie, who sips, makes a face, and hands it back.

ROSALIE
That's terrible.

CALEB
Rosalie.

FATHER EMILIO
She is right. It is terrible coffee. But it is warm.

The sun touches the horizon. The shadows of the walls stretch over the three of them. The mule shifts its weight and sighs.

CALEB
Father, I came out to scatter my wife's ashes. I thought this was the place.

FATHER EMILIO
Why here?

CALEB
She was married here. Fifty years ago in June.

FATHER EMILIO
Then it is the place.

Caleb takes a small tin from inside his coat. He turns it over in his hands. Rosalie puts down her stick and takes his free hand. The priest crosses himself and stands.

FATHER EMILIO (CONT'D)
Shall I say something?

CALEB
Please.

FATHER EMILIO
(gently)
Lord, here is a woman who loved this land. Let her rest in it.

Caleb opens the tin. A breeze rises across the courtyard, and with it the first of the ashes lifts, a pale ribbon curling over the dry fountain, and then drifting out through the gap in the wall toward the last light.

ROSALIE
(whispering)
Bye, Grandma.

The wind lifts her hat. The mule lifts his head. The three of them watch the ribbon of dust until it is gone.
